 ## Background
-The method was introduced by Elek et al. in 2013 as a screen-space multiple-scattering approximation [S1][S2, unofficial]. It builds on the mathematical path integration framework introduced by Tesfa [Tes87] and Perez et al. [PAS03] to describe multiple scattering effects in terms of spatial and angular blurring [S1]. The specific formula for the standard deviation of the Gaussian spatial-spreading distribution for a collimated beam was derived by Premože et al. [2004] [S2, unofficial]. The method was developed to address the computational expense of volumetric Monte Carlo and finite element techniques, which can take hours to days to solve [S1].
+The method was introduced by Elek et al. in 2013 as a screen-space multiple-scattering approximation [S1][S3, unofficial]. It builds on the mathematical path integration framework introduced by Tesfa [Tes87] and Perez et al. [PAS03] to describe multiple scattering effects in terms of spatial and angular blurring [S2]. The specific formula for the standard deviation of the Gaussian spatial-spreading distribution for a collimated beam was derived by Premože et al. [2004] [S3, unofficial]. The method was developed to address the computational expense of volumetric Monte Carlo and finite element techniques, which can take hours to days to solve [S1].
 
